## 3. Exact Modeling Under Physical Law: Tractability by Computational Univalence and Witnessed Erasure

### 3.1 Complexity discipline: worst-case hardness vs. structured physical families

A rigorous literature review of exact electronic structure complexity is already recorded in `docs/chemistry-open-problems.md`:
- **Worst-case hardness is proved:** 2-local Hamiltonians are QMA-complete (Kitaev; Kempe, Kitaev & Regev 2006), even in 1D (Aharonov, Gottesman, Irani & Kempe 2007); the Hubbard model in a site-dependent magnetic field is QMA-hard (Schuch & Verstraete 2009); fixed-basis electronic structure with fixed electron number is QMA-complete, and Hartree–Fock is NP-complete (O'Gorman, Irani, Whitfield & Fefferman 2022). Analogously, general HP-lattice protein folding is NP-complete, and general dynamical-system inversion is intractable.
- **Tractability is proved wherever physical structure exists:** Hastings (2007) proved an area law for gapped 1D local Hamiltonians; Landau, Vazirani & Vidick (2015) gave a provably polynomial-time algorithm for their ground states via polynomial-bond-dimension MPS. The spectral gap, locality, and bounded entanglement across cuts are the structural properties that collapse exponential Hilbert spaces.

The conclusion in `docs/chemistry-open-problems.md` is exact: **no contraction makes the unstructured worst-case problem efficient unless QMA collapses; contraction along mathematical identities makes a physical family tractable exactly when the family carries structure (a gap, locality, symmetry, minimal frustration, isopotentiality), and the proof of tractability is a bound on the quotient.**

